<body>
  <!-- ============ Sticky chapter bar ============ -->
  <nav class="bar" id="bar" aria-label="Chapters">
    <div class="bar-in">
      <a class="bar-home" href="#top" aria-label="Back to top"><img src="/images/lotus.png" alt="" width="24" height="24" /></a>
      <ol>
        {CHAPTERS.map((c) => (
          <li><a href={`#${c.id}`} data-ch={c.id}><span class="n">{c.n}</span>{c.short}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="prog" id="prog"></div>
  </nav>

  <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
  <section class="hero" id="top">
    <div class="wide hero-in">
      <div>
        <img class="logo" src="/images/logo-orange.png" alt="Flourish Legal Well-Being" width="168" />
        <h1>&ldquo;Associates Don&rsquo;t Want to Work Anymore&rdquo;</h1>
        <p class="sub-title">Helping Partners Motivate Today&rsquo;s Associates</p>
        <p class="from">A playbook from the NALP webinar, with Jon Krop of <a href="https://teamflourish.com">Flourish</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="chat" role="group" aria-label="Chat from the webinar">
        <div class="chat-bar"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Chat</div>
        <p class="chat-q">&ldquo;In the past few months, have you heard a partner say some version of &lsquo;Associates just don&rsquo;t want to work anymore&rsquo;? Drop a yes in the chat.&rdquo;</p>
        <div class="chat-feed">
          <span class="msg yes" style="animation-delay:.4s">yes</span>
          <span class="msg yes" style="animation-delay:.55s">Yes!</span>
          <span class="msg yes" style="animation-delay:.7s">yes</span>
          <span class="msg yes" style="animation-delay:.8s">YES</span>
          <span class="msg yes" style="animation-delay:.9s">yes</span>
          <span class="msg long" style="animation-delay:1.1s">&ldquo;Associates want to work as little as they can get away with.&rdquo;</span>
          <span class="msg yes" style="animation-delay:1.25s">yes</span>
          <span class="msg yes" style="animation-delay:1.35s">yes</span>
          <span class="msg long" style="animation-delay:1.55s">&ldquo;Associates don&rsquo;t work weekends anymore.&rdquo;</span>
          <span class="msg yes" style="animation-delay:1.7s">yep</span>
          <span class="msg yes" style="animation-delay:1.8s">yes</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main>
  <!-- ============ Intro ============ -->
  <section class="intro">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="big">It&rsquo;s not entirely partners&rsquo; imagination. Something has shifted. But associates aren&rsquo;t simply less willing to work. They just need to be engaged differently.</p>
      <p class="txt">Getting partners there takes care, because the topic makes them defensive fast. Here are the five principles we use in our partner program, <b>How to Inspire A-Game Performance</b>, and why they work, so you can use them in all the ways you work with partners.</p>
      <p class="legend"><span class="chip" aria-hidden="true"></span>Lines in orange are yours to borrow, word for word.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="contents" aria-label="The five principles">
    <div class="wide">
      <div class="toc-head">The five principles</div>
      <ol class="cards5">
        {CHAPTERS.slice(0, 5).map((c) => (
          <li><a href={`#${c.id}`}><span class="n">{c.n}</span><span class="t">{c.title}</span></a></li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <div class="contents-tools">
        <a class="btn solid" href="#share">&darr; A resource page to share with partners</a>
        <a class="btn" href="#toolkit">The toolkit: each method in detail</a>
        <button class="btn" type="button" onclick="window.print()">Print or save as PDF</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- ============ 1 ============ -->
  <section class="principle alt" id="commiserate">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h2>Commiserate first</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">Before you try to teach partners anything, commiserate with them.</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>Name their problem, show you see it, and make clear you&rsquo;re here to help. <b>Feeling understood is what makes them receptive.</b></li>
        <li>Partners don&rsquo;t think they need training. But they know they have problems, so lead with the problem.</li>
        <li>Not sure how they see it? Ask.</li>
        <li>Aisha Greene, Deputy Chief Talent Officer at Hogan Lovells Cadwalader, never asks partners what training they want. She asks this, then uses their exact language:</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="borrow-line">&ldquo;What could I do to help you be more valuable to your clients?&rdquo;</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>Frame everything around their self-interest.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 2 ============ -->
  <section class="principle" id="frameworks">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">2</span><h2>Use simple, memorable frameworks</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">Partners are very smart. They&rsquo;re also incredibly busy, with a ton already on their minds.</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>If you want something to stick, boil it down to a simple, memorable framework.</li>
        <li>For example, <b>TRIP</b>, for making a difficult ask. Put four things in it, in any order:</li>
      </ul>
      <ol class="trip4">
        <li><span class="mk" aria-hidden="true">T</span><span><b>Thank</b> them.</span></li>
        <li><span class="mk" aria-hidden="true">R</span><span>Give a <b>Reason</b>, and why now.</span></li>
        <li><span class="mk" aria-hidden="true">I</span><span>Acknowledge the <b>Impact</b> on them.</span></li>
        <li><span class="mk" aria-hidden="true">P</span><span>Include some <b>Praise</b>.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(900px + 2*var(--gutter))">
      <div class="emails">
        <div class="ba-col"><p class="ba-label">Before</p>
        <div class="email before">
          <div class="ebar"><span>New message</span></div>
          <div class="meta">To: Associate</div>
          <div class="ebody">Need this tonight, thanks.</div>
        </div></div>
        <div class="ba-col"><p class="ba-label after">After</p>
        <div class="email after">
          <div class="ebar"><span>New message</span></div>
          <div class="meta">To: Associate</div>
          <div class="ebody">
            <span class="seg"><span class="lt">I: Impact</span><span>I know this might mess up your evening,</span></span>
            <span class="seg"><span class="lt">R: Reason</span><span>but the client moved up their deadline and it took us by surprise.</span></span>
            <span class="seg"><span class="lt">P: Praise</span><span>I&rsquo;m coming to you because I know you have the skills to handle this the right way.</span></span>
            <span class="seg"><span class="lt">T: Thanks</span><span>Thanks for your help.</span></span>
          </div>
        </div></div>
      </div>
      <p class="cap">An example partners can use with associates. It&rsquo;s an extra two minutes, and it makes all the difference in the world.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
      <ul class="pts" style="margin-top:32px">
        <li>Other simple, memorable frameworks we use are in the <a href="#toolkit">toolkit below</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 3 ============ -->
  <section class="principle alt" id="blame">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">3</span><h2>Don&rsquo;t blame them</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">Partners complain about associates&rsquo; work. But show them how to get better work, and they hear: &ldquo;Oh, so you&rsquo;re saying it&rsquo;s my fault.&rdquo;</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>So avoid anything that could even be <b>construed</b> as blaming them. Here are the two places they&rsquo;re most likely to feel blamed, and how we frame each.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(980px + 2*var(--gutter))">
      <div class="cards2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>When their approach isn&rsquo;t resonating</h3>
          <p class="hears">What they might hear: &ldquo;It&rsquo;s your fault the associates aren&rsquo;t responding.&rdquo;</p>
          <p>We teach partners to <a href="#tool-invest"><b>invest before you withdraw</b></a>: because today&rsquo;s associates see their work through their working relationships, it&rsquo;s important to make periodic &ldquo;deposits&rdquo; in the &ldquo;account&rdquo; of the relationship, especially before a withdrawal like a weekend or a difficult ask. But we don&rsquo;t want partners to feel judged for not perceiving this dynamic. So we say, right away:</p>
          <p class="borrow-line">&ldquo;If this doesn&rsquo;t feel intuitive, we completely understand. It wasn&rsquo;t like that for you. It&rsquo;s not your fault. But if you learn to speak this language, it will help you get what you want.&rdquo;</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>When they&rsquo;ve been harsh</h3>
          <p class="hears">What they might hear: &ldquo;You were too harsh with your associate.&rdquo;</p>
          <p>A curt email, a short answer in a meeting. Raise it directly and they get defensive. So we frame it as <b>stress spillover</b>:</p>
          <p class="borrow-line">&ldquo;We understand you&rsquo;re almost always under pressure, and sometimes that stress just spills over. It&rsquo;s not malice. It&rsquo;s unintentional, and it&rsquo;s very human.&rdquo;</p>
          <p>That softens it enough to land. Then we offer the fix: <a href="#tool-pause">one before speaking, three before sending</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 4 ============ -->
  <section class="principle" id="objections">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">4</span><h2>Address objections. Better yet, preempt them.</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">The best time to answer an objection is before anyone raises it.</p>
      <p class="move-h">When an objection does come up, never argue head-on. Make two moves:</p>
      <div class="move-steps">
        <div class="step"><span class="mv">1</span><div><b>Commiserate</b>Agree with what&rsquo;s true. There&rsquo;s almost always a kernel of truth in what they&rsquo;re saying.</div></div>
        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="step"><span class="mv">2</span><div><b>Go to their self-interest</b>Focus on what&rsquo;s in it for them.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="dialog">
        <div class="ex">
          <div class="bub they"><span class="who">Partner</span>&ldquo;I already pay them. Shouldn&rsquo;t I just get the work?&rdquo;</div>
          <div class="bub you"><span class="who">You</span>&ldquo;That&rsquo;s totally reasonable; you do pay them well. But pay will get you compliance. If compliance is all you want, pay is all you need. But if you want their A-game, that&rsquo;s not the currency that gets it.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ex">
          <div class="bub they"><span class="who">Partner</span>&ldquo;Why is this all on me? Are you talking to the associates too?&rdquo;</div>
          <div class="bub you"><span class="who">You</span>&ldquo;You&rsquo;re right, it&rsquo;s not just on you. We&rsquo;re working with the associates too.&rdquo;</div>
          <p class="note">Partners always ask this one, and this is the only answer that keeps their buy-in. If your firm does associate programming on managing up, <b>say so, early and often,</b> before they ask.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ex">
          <div class="bub they"><span class="who">Senior partner</span>&ldquo;It&rsquo;s the nature of the game.&rdquo;</div>
          <div class="bub you"><span class="who">You</span>&ldquo;You&rsquo;re right. Late nights are part of the job. So when you need one, do you want their A-game, or their B, C, or D game?&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ex">
          <div class="bub they"><span class="who">Partner</span>&ldquo;Clients don&rsquo;t coddle us. Why should we coddle associates?&rdquo;</div>
          <div class="bub you"><span class="who">You</span>&ldquo;You&rsquo;re right that they need to be ready for how clients treat us. Could we prepare them another way, like bringing them onto client calls earlier?&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ex">
          <div class="bub they"><span class="who">Partner</span>&ldquo;One strike and you&rsquo;re out.&rdquo;</div>
          <div class="bub you"><span class="who">You</span>&ldquo;Look, clients pay for excellence, and high standards matter. But every associate you cut shrinks your bench and puts more work on your desk. What if you tried one round of feedback first? I&rsquo;m happy to workshop it with you. If that doesn&rsquo;t work, no one&rsquo;s going to stand in your way.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="biggest">
      <div class="col" style="max-width:calc(760px + 2*var(--gutter))">
        <p class="q">The biggest objection: &ldquo;So you&rsquo;re asking me to lower my standards?&rdquo;</p>
        <p>Kill the &ldquo;coddling&rdquo; reading before it comes up. Here&rsquo;s how we like to do it, with a line I stole from my friend Urs Koenig, a leadership coach:</p>
        <div class="ans">
          <img src="/images/partners-listen/urs-koenig.webp" alt="Leadership coach Urs Koenig" width="480" height="480" loading="lazy" />
          <p class="big">&ldquo;Tough on results, tender on people.&rdquo;</p>
        </div>
        <p>We say it at the very start of our program, and make clear we&rsquo;re never asking partners to lower their standards.</p>
        <p>In fact, <b>hollow praise is actually a withdrawal</b> from the relationship account. Thoughtful critique, from someone trying to help them do better next time, is a deposit.</p>
        <p>Here&rsquo;s one sentence partners can add to their critiques. In &ldquo;wise feedback&rdquo; studies (mostly with students), critique was acted on more often when it was added:</p>
        <p class="wise">&ldquo;I&rsquo;m giving you these comments because I have very high expectations and I know that you can reach them.&rdquo;</p>
        <p class="tie">It&rsquo;s a perfect example of how a partner can be tough on results, tender on people.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 5 ============ -->
  <section class="principle alt" id="time">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">5</span><h2>Minimize the time commitment</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">&ldquo;I know you&rsquo;re busy. I&rsquo;m here to deliver simplicity.&rdquo;</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t have time&rdquo; is the objection you&rsquo;ll hear most. That line is how we open with partners.</li>
        <li>Ask for small adjustments to things they already do. If a change can&rsquo;t be done in two minutes, shrink it until it can.</li>
        <li>Offload the remembering: a recurring calendar reminder, ideally set up by their assistant. <b>Assistants are often your best allies.</b> Our partner program also includes an app that drafts TRIP asks and recognition notes.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Toolkit ============ -->
  <section class="toolkit" id="toolkit">
    <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(980px + 2*var(--gutter))">
      <div class="kit-head">
        <h2>The toolkit: each method, in detail</h2>
        <p>This is how we teach them to partners. Each is also on a page written for partners, which you can forward.</p>
        <ol class="kit-index">
          <li><a href="#tool-invest">Invest before you withdraw</a></li>
          <li><a href="#tool-pause">One before speaking, three before sending</a></li>
          <li><a href="#tool-trip">TRIP</a></li>
          <li><a href="#tool-ladder">The Recognition Ladder</a></li>
          <li><a href="#tool-ins">The Three In&rsquo;s</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <a class="dl" id="share" href={PARTNER_PAGE} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <img src="/images/partners-listen/thumb-partner-page.jpg" alt="" width="800" height="342" loading="lazy" />
        <div>
          <span class="name">A resource page to share with partners</span>
          <span class="desc">All five methods, written for partners. Just forward the link.</span>
        </div>
        <span class="go">Open the page &rarr;</span>
      </a>

      <div class="tool" id="tool-invest">
        <div class="tool-meta">
          <h3>Invest before you withdraw</h3>
          <span class="for">The principle underneath everything</span>
          <a class="sheet" href={PARTNER_PAGE + "#invest"} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3h7v7M13 3L4 12"/></svg>On the partner page</a>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-body">
          <p>Every interaction is a deposit or a withdrawal. Before a hard ask, make sure there are enough deposits to absorb it.</p>
          <p>A deposit is anything that makes the associate feel <b>seen</b> as a person, <b>valued</b> for their contribution, or <b>trusted</b> with responsibility. It only counts if they perceive it. And it isn&rsquo;t the same as being nice: hollow praise makes them feel patronized, while honest, specific critique can be a deposit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool" id="tool-pause">
        <div class="tool-meta">
          <h3>One before speaking, three before sending</h3>
          <span class="for">For keeping stress from spilling over</span>
          <a class="sheet" href={PARTNER_PAGE + "#spillover"} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3h7v7M13 3L4 12"/></svg>On the partner page</a>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-body">
          <div class="breath" aria-hidden="true">
            <div><span class="count"><i></i></span><b>One breath</b><span>before speaking</span></div>
            <div><span class="count"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><b>Three breaths</b><span>before sending</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>Feeling stressed or keyed up, even a little? Take <b>one breath before speaking</b> and <b>three before sending an email.</b> Why one? Three breaths mid-conversation would seem weird. Why three? You have the time, and email leaves a record.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool" id="tool-trip">
        <div class="tool-meta">
          <h3>TRIP</h3>
          <span class="for">For making a difficult ask</span>
          <a class="sheet" href={PARTNER_PAGE + "#trip"} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3h7v7M13 3L4 12"/></svg>On the partner page</a>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-body">
          <ul class="letters">
            <li><span class="mk">T</span><span><b>Thank them</b>It goes a long way.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">R</span><span><b>Give a reason</b>Why are you asking for this, and why now?</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">I</span><span><b>Acknowledge the impact</b>Show that you see what this costs them.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">P</span><span><b>Include praise</b>Affirm what makes them right for this ask.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <div class="watch"><div class="wk">Common misses</div><ul>
            <li><b>Praise that insults everyone else.</b> <i>&ldquo;You&rsquo;re the most thorough researcher on the team&rdquo;</i> just demoted the rest of the team.</li>
            <li><b>Over-apologizing.</b> Five sentences of &ldquo;I feel awful&rdquo; makes the ask about the partner.</li>
            <li><b>&ldquo;Get back to me if you can.&rdquo;</b> Don&rsquo;t give them an option you don&rsquo;t want them to have. Give a deadline.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool" id="tool-ladder">
        <div class="tool-meta">
          <h3>The Recognition Ladder</h3>
          <span class="for">For recognizing effort</span>
          <a class="sheet" href={PARTNER_PAGE + "#ladder"} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3h7v7M13 3L4 12"/></svg>On the partner page</a>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-body">
          <p>Work that gets no recognition can hit them as a withdrawal: the partner has moved on, but the associate hasn&rsquo;t. Each rung asks a little more of the partner.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-wide">
          <ol class="ladder">
            <li class="rung"><span class="r">1</span><b>Praise privately</b>Thirty seconds. Be specific: &ldquo;The way you structured the estoppel argument in section III, that was sharp&rdquo; beats &ldquo;great work.&rdquo;</li>
            <li class="rung"><span class="r">2</span><b>Praise publicly</b>The deal-close email, the practice group meeting. Skip it if others contributed equally and aren&rsquo;t named.</li>
            <li class="rung"><span class="r">3</span><b>Praise upwardly</b>To the practice group leader or the client. CC the associate so they know.</li>
            <li class="rung"><span class="r">4</span><b>Entrust with responsibility</b>&ldquo;I want you to take the next depo.&rdquo; You&rsquo;re showing trust by handing them something real.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="ladder-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>Asks less</span><span class="line"></span><span>Asks more</span></div>
          <div class="watch"><div class="wk">Check the fit</div><ul>
            <li>Higher isn&rsquo;t always better. A shy associate may dread public praise. Someone who just worked four straight weekends may hear &ldquo;more responsibility&rdquo; as punishment.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool" id="tool-ins">
        <div class="tool-meta">
          <h3>The Three In&rsquo;s</h3>
          <span class="for">For small, steady deposits</span>
          <a class="sheet" href={PARTNER_PAGE + "#ins"} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3h7v7M13 3L4 12"/></svg>On the partner page</a>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-body">
          <ul class="ins">
            <li><div class="w"><span>In</span>troduction</div>Connect them to someone worth knowing: another partner, a mentor, someone at the client.</li>
            <li><div class="w"><span>In</span>vitation</div>Bring them into a client call, a strategy meeting, witness prep, or just lunch.</li>
            <li><div class="w"><span>In</span>terest</div>No need to memorize their hobbies. &ldquo;I know the last week&rsquo;s been busy. How are you holding up?&rdquo; is a partner-sized display of interest.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="watch"><div class="wk">Common misses</div><ul>
            <li>Forward an article on &ldquo;ten habits of successful lawyers,&rdquo; and they may hear: my partner thinks I need to improve.</li>
            <li>Invite them to a client call, then do all the talking, and they spend an hour feeling invisible.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Start ============ -->
  <section class="start">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="big">Where to start: one partner, one principle, this week.</p>
      <p>You don&rsquo;t have to do all of this at once. Take one principle into your next partner conversation, or pass along one tool.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  </main>

